# lit-legs.ps1 stops when it cannot read $p3Tests (plan 5.17.6, M24; the
# self-test M26 adds, plan section 12): it builds its legs with the lines of
# 'check-matrix.py --p3-tests <repo>', and when the checker fails, or prints
# a line that is not a repository-relative test path under msil/test, no leg
# may run without $p3Tests (G6 runs the same checker, so a broken checker
# must not pass a gate silently). MSIL_PYTHON is a stand-in Python
# (Inputs/ci-legs.py python) that runs the real one on every command line
# except check-matrix.py, which it plays as MSIL_FAKE_P3 says. Against the
# stand-in build of Inputs/ci-legs.py, whose lit records its calls:
# (1) The stand-in itself changes nothing: with MSIL_FAKE_P3 unset, -Describe
# succeeds with the legs that Inputs/legs-check.py accepts, and the stand-in
# saw the checker's command line.
# (2) A failing checker (exit 1, a stderr and a stdout line): -Describe and
# '-Leg harness' exit 1 with 'check-matrix.py --p3-tests exited 1, so the legs
# cannot take $p3Tests (plan 5.17.6): <message>', where <message> is the
# checker's stderr and stdout lines joined with '; ', print no JSON and start
# no lit; a checker that exits 3 without output gives 'no message'.
# (3) A printed line that is not a test path under msil/test (a path outside
# it, or one through '..') stops the script with its own message
# ('check-matrix.py --p3-tests printed '<line>', which is not a
# repository-relative test path under msil/test'), and starts no lit.
# (4) -List does not read $p3Tests: it still succeeds with a failing checker.
# Each check pins its failure path: the planted checker is the fault, and
# (1) is the run without it.
# REQUIRES: msil-ci-pwsh

# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/ci-legs.py setup %t/build
# RUN: %python %S/Inputs/ci-legs.py python %t/py > %t/py.txt
# DEFINE: %{stop_py} = %t/py/python.cmd

